creasing and then decreasing.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    if n == 1:\n        return arr[0]\n\n    # Calculate LIS sums ending at each position\n    lis_sums = [0] * n\n    for i in range(n):\n        lis_sums[i] = arr[i]\n        for j in range(i):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j]:\n                lis_sums[i] = max(lis_sums[i], lis_sums[j] + arr[i])\n\n    # Calculate LDS sums starting at each position (by reversing and finding LIS)\n    # This is equivalent to LIS on the reversed array, then reversing the DP array\n    lds_sums = [0] * n\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        lds_sums[i] = arr[i]\n        for j in range(n - 1, i, -1):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j]:\n                lds_sums[i] = max(lds_sums[i], lds_sums[j] + arr[i])\n\n    # Combine LIS and LDS sums to find the maximum bitonic sum\n    # A bitonic subsequence must have a peak.\n    # The peak element is counted in both LIS and LDS sums, so subtract it once.\n    max_bitonic_sum = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        # A bitonic sequence must have at least one increasing part and one decreasing part to be truly bitonic\n        # (unless it's purely increasing or purely decreasing, which means the other part is of length 1).\n        # However, the problem definition \"first increasing and then decreasing\" implies a peak.\n        # If lis_sums[i] == arr[i] and lds_sums[i] == arr[i], it means arr[i] can only form a sequence of length 1.\n        # For a valid bitonic sequence (length > 1), at least one side must have length > 0\n        # If it's a strictly increasing sequence, lds_sums[i] would just be arr[i].\n        # If it's a strictly decreasing sequence, lis_sums[i] would just be arr[i].\n        # The sum is (sum of increasing part ending at i) + (sum of decreasing part starting at i) - arr[i] (to remove peak duplication).\n        # We need to ensure that the bitonic sequence is not purely increasing or purely decreasing (unless n=1).\n        # A valid peak `i` requires that it could potentially be part of an increasing sequence leading to it AND a decreasing sequence starting from it.\n        # A peak `i` implies `arr[j] < arr[i]` for some `j < i` (increasing part) AND `arr[k] < arr[i]` for some `k > i` (decreasing part).\n        # If either `lis_sums[i] == arr[i]` or `lds_sums[i] == arr[i]`, it implies `arr[i]` can only be a standalone increasing/decreasing part.\n        # This condition is tricky. Instead, simply iterate and assume `arr[i]` is the peak.\n        # If lis_sums[i] and lds_sums[i] both refer to arr[i] as the sole element, then (arr[i] + arr[i] - arr[i]) = arr[i].\n        # The maximum value needs to be > arr[i] for a non-trivial bitonic sequence.\n\n        current_sum = lis_sums[i] + lds_sums[i] - arr[i]\n        max_bitonic_sum = max(max_bitonic_sum, current_sum)\n\n    # Consider purely increasing or purely decreasing sequences as degenerate bitonic sequences.\n    # The problem description \"first increasing and then decreasing\" implies a peak and segments on both sides.\n    # If the max_bitonic_sum is still 0 (e.g., all elements are equal or strictly decreasing/increasing),\n    # then this might not be sufficient. However, for a single element array, it would be arr[0].\n    # The LIS sums and LDS sums already account for this, as lis_sums[i] is at least arr[i], and same for lds_sums.\n    # So `max_bitonic_sum` will be at least `max(arr)` if n > 0.\n    # This covers the cases where a sequence is purely increasing (peak at the end) or purely decreasing (peak at the beginning),\n    # where one of the `lis_sums[i]` or `lds_sums[i]` will be just `arr[i]`.\n    # For example, for [1, 2, 3], LIS sums = [1, 3, 6], LDS sums = [1, 2, 3] (if processed from right: [3, 2, 1]).\n    # Correct LDS for [1,2,3] is: [1,2,3] -> rev: [3,2,1] -> LIS: [3, 5, 6] -> rev: [6,5,3].\n    # So for [1,2,3], LIS_sums = [1,3,6], LDS_sums = [3,5,6].\n    #\n    # Test case: [1, 15, 51, 45, 33, 100, 1